WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.320
Defenders usually describe DDoS events in three broad categories because each category

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:07.380
points to different signals,

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.600
owners, and controls.

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:13.020
Volumetric events are about scale against capacity.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:18.200
They attempt to consume available bandwidth, edge throughput, or provider-facing capacity.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.180
From a defensive perspective,

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.880
the important question is whether traffic is saturating the path before it reaches the

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:26.140
application.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:31.960
If the pipe, edge, or provider handoff is overwhelmed, local server tuning may not matter.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:36.340
The response usually requires upstream visibility, edge protection,

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:38.160
or provider assistance.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:43.980
Protocol or state exhaustion events target the ability of infrastructure to track and

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:45.480
process traffic.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:51.060
The affected component might be a firewall, load balancer, connection table, proxy,

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:52.960
or other stateful device.

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:58.060
The defensive question is whether connection rates, half-open sessions, table pressure,

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:01.080
or device saturation are limiting service.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:05.380
Controls in this category must be careful because they can affect legitimate users,

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.800
monitoring, partner systems, and recovery paths.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:12.940
The right control is usually scoped, observable, and reversible.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:17.720
Application-layer events are different because the traffic may look closer to ordinary web

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:19.220
or API use.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:25.200
The pressure appears in expensive routes, login flows, search features, dynamic pages,

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:30.060
payment paths, or APIs that require database, cache, or downstream work.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:36.760
The network may not look saturated, but latency, errors, queue depth, CPU, memory,

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:39.700
database load, or thread pools can climb.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.400
Application-layer defense often needs security, SRE, platform,

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:49.820
and application teams together because the best control may be a WAF rule, a cache change,

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.640
a quota, a circuit breaker, or a temporary degraded mode.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:56.820
Classification is not about assigning a dramatic label.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.120
It is about choosing the right next move.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:02.140
A volumetric event may need provider escalation.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:06.680
A state exhaustion event may need connection and upstream controls.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:11.700
An application-layer event may need route-specific protection and business prioritization.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.000
Good defenders avoid overclaiming.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:17.580
They describe what they can observe, state the confidence level,

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:21.800
and keep the response focused on availability, recovery, and evidence.
